tatically\n- Less dynamic reallocation\n- Generally the more conservative/stable option\n- Often the recommended default for most workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **CCS disabled**: This effectively disables CCS context handling entirely\n- All work is routed through the primary compute engines\n- **When to use it:**\n  - **Debugging driver issues** related to CCS context management\n  - **Stability problems** where CCS modes 1 or 2 cause crashes or incorrect results\n  - **Compatibility** with certain older applications or specific workloads that don't play well with CCS\n  - **Troubleshooting** GPU hangs or artifacts that may be CCS-related\n\n## Practical Guidance\n\nFor most users, **mode 2** is a good default. You'd typically reach for **mode 4** only when:\n- Experiencing instability that you suspect is CCS-related\n- Debugging driver bugs\n- A specific workload shows better behavior with CCS disabled\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I'm providing this based on my understanding of the Xe driver architecture, but driver parameters can vary between kernel versions and specific driver releases. I'd recommend:\n\n1. Checking your specific driver version's documentation\n2. Testing performance/stability differences empirically with your actual workloads\n3. Consulting the latest"}